course: biology
lesson: 315
title: Bats near home: pipistrelles, noctules, bat boxes and why never to pick one up
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On a summer evening in Britain, a tiny shape flits round and round a street lamp. It is not a moth or a bird but a pipistrelle, a bat lighter than a pound coin, snapping up insects drawn to the light.
explain: About 17 kinds of bat breed in Britain. They hunt by sound, shouting clicks too high for us to hear and listening for the echoes; a bat detector turns those clicks into sounds we can hear.
explain: British bats raise one baby a year in summer roosts in roofs and trees, and sleep through winter in cold places. Because they lose roosts easily, the law protects them and their roosts.
explain: Bats are gentle and useful, but a bat you find on the ground needs a trained helper, never bare hands: tell a grown-up, who can call a bat helpline or a vet.
fact: The common pipistrelle is Britain's commonest bat; it weighs about 5 grams, less than a pound coin, may eat thousands of tiny insects in one night, and can squeeze through a gap about 1 centimetre wide.
fact: In the 1990s scientists noticed some pipistrelles called at a higher pitch than others; studies showed they were two kinds, the common and the soprano pipistrelle.
fact: The noctule is one of Britain's biggest bats; it flies high and fast over the treetops, often before it is fully dark, roosts in old woodpecker holes, and its calls are low enough that some children can hear them.
fact: When a bat closes in on an insect, its clicks speed up into a buzz that can be well over a hundred clicks a second, because more clicks give more echoes and a sharper picture of where the insect is.
fact: A bat box is a wooden box with a narrow slot at the bottom; it should be put high up, out of reach of cats, catching some sun, and boxes are often put in groups facing different ways so bats can choose warmer or cooler ones.
fact: In Britain it is against the law to disturb roosting bats or open a bat box without a licence; trained bat workers with licences check the boxes and count the bats.
fact: A bat on the ground in daytime may be hurt, cold, tired or a lost baby; never pick it up with bare hands, because some British bats carry a rare rabies-like virus and only trained, vaccinated people should handle them.
fact: A bat caught by a cat needs a rescuer even if it looks fine, because tiny wounds from cat teeth can become infected and kill the bat days later; cats catch many thousands of bats in Britain each year, reported.
fact: British bats hibernate in winter when insects disappear, slowing their hearts and cooling down; waking them uses up the fat they need to last until spring.
fact: In summer, mother bats gather in roofs and walls to raise their babies, each usually having only one baby a year, so losing one roost can hurt a whole local group.
fact: The greater horseshoe bat has a nose leaf shaped like a horseshoe that beams out its calls; some bats call through their noses and others through open mouths.
fact: Bats do not get tangled in people's hair; a bat swooping near your head is most likely catching insects flying around you, and its echoes can dodge a thread.
exercise: Which is Britain's commonest bat? | The common pipistrelle | The noctule | The vampire bat of the woods | The big fruit-eating flying fox
exercise: About how much does a common pipistrelle weigh? | About 5 grams, less than a pound coin | About 500 grams, as heavy as a big tin of beans | About 50 grams, about as heavy as an egg | About 5 kilograms, as heavy as a big cat
exercise: Why does a pipistrelle fly round a street lamp at dusk? | To catch the insects drawn to the light | To warm its cold wings near the hot lamp | Because the bright street light blinds it | To find a mate in the warm glow of the light
exercise: How did scientists find that there were two kinds of pipistrelle? | Some called at a higher pitch than others | Some had bright green fur on their backs | Some had three wings | Some flew only in the coldest weeks of winter
exercise: Where does a noctule roost? | In old woodpecker holes in trees | In old rabbit burrows under hedges | Under the sea | In birds' eggs
exercise: Why can some children hear a noctule but adults cannot? | Its calls are low enough for young ears that hear high sounds better | Children have much bigger ears than adults for the size of their heads | Adults are always too busy | Noctules only talk to children
exercise: Why do a bat's clicks speed up into a buzz as it catches an insect? | More clicks give more echoes, showing exactly where the insect is | It is getting tired | It is singing a happy song to celebrate its catch | It is scaring other bats away
exercise: What does a bat detector do? | Turns bat calls into sounds we can hear | Catches bats in a soft net at night as they fly | Shines a bright light that bats follow home | Counts the bats' tiny sharp teeth one by one
exercise: Where should a bat box be put? | High up, out of reach of cats, catching some sun | On the ground under a thick bush, where it is dark | Inside a fridge | Under water in a pond
exercise: Why are bat boxes often put in groups facing different ways? | So bats can choose a warmer or cooler box | So the bats can watch each other through the holes | To catch more rain | To confuse owls
exercise: Who may open a bat box in Britain? | Trained bat workers with a licence | Anyone at all who is curious about bats | Any child on a school trip with a teacher | Only the person who owns the tree or garden
exercise: What should you do if you find a bat on the ground in daytime? | Not touch it; tell a grown-up, who can call a bat helpline or vet | Pick it up and throw it high into the air so that it can fly off home | Take it to bed to keep it warm | Give it milk to drink
exercise: Why should nobody pick up a bat with bare hands? | Some carry a rare rabies-like virus and they may bite when scared | Bats are made of glass | Bats always explode when touched by a warm hand | Bat fur gives you warts
exercise: Why does a bat caught by a cat need help even if it looks fine? | Tiny wounds from cat teeth can get infected and kill it later | The cat will want to play with it again tomorrow | Cats make bats forget how to fly for ever and ever | It will turn into a cat
exercise: Why should cavers keep away from hibernating bats? | Waking them uses fat they need to last the winter | Bats wake up angry and chase them out of the cave | Hibernating bats glow and blind people | The bats would wake and fly south
exercise: How many babies does a British mother bat usually have each year? | One | Ten or so | Twenty | Five
exercise: About how many kinds of bat breed in Britain? | About 17 | Only about 2 | About 170 | About 1,400
exercise: What does a horseshoe bat's nose leaf do? | Beams out its calls | Smells for blood in the dark | Catches rain to drink | Holds onto thin branches
test: «a bat nearly got stuck in my hair!!» What is true? | Bats do not tangle in hair; it was probably catching insects around you | Bats nest in hair, so wear a hat | Bats aim for hair to steal it for their nests, so they can keep their babies warm | Bats are blind and crash into heads
test: «there's a bat on the patio in daylight, can i just pick it up and put it in a box» What should happen? | Do not touch it; tell a grown-up, who calls a bat helpline or vet | Yes, pick it up and cuddle it | Yes, then throw it up so it can fly | Leave it out in the sun to warm up for a week, then it will fly away
test: «the cat dragged in a bat but it flew off fine, so all good yeah?» What is right? | Not really; bats caught by cats need a rescuer, as tiny wounds can kill later | Yes, if it flies it can never be hurt | Yes, cat teeth are clean | Yes, bats heal any wound in a minute, even a very deep bite from a cat or a dog
test: Your mate wants to open the bat box to show his little brother. What should you say? | No, opening it disturbs roosting bats, which is against the law without a licence | Yes, bats love visitors in the day | Yes, but only shake it a little | Yes, if you knock on the box first and wait for the bats to come out and say hello
test: Count them: a summer roost has 40 mother bats, and each usually has one baby. About how many babies are born? | About 40 | About 400 | About 80 | About 4
test: Count them: 6 pipistrelles each weigh about 5 grams. About how much do they weigh together? | About 30 grams | About 11 grams | About 300 grams | About 3 grams
test: What would happen to a local group of bats if a house owner sealed up the roof where they raise their babies? | The mothers would lose their nursery, and with one baby a year the group could shrink | Nothing, bats raise babies anywhere | The bats would simply lay their eggs outside instead, in little nests in the garden hedge | They would move into the fridge
test: In a cartoon Batman's bats live in a dark cave and all hang there all year round. What do real British bats do? | Raise babies in summer roosts like roofs and trees, and hibernate somewhere cool in winter | Live in one cave for ever and never move | Sleep all summer and fly all winter | Fly south to Africa every winter like swallows, and come back to Britain each spring to roost
test: You walk with a bat detector and the steady clicks suddenly become a fast buzz. What just happened? | The bat was closing in on an insect | The batteries ran out | The bat fell asleep in the air | A big bird swooped and scared the bat away
test: «why would a bat pick a sunny box some days and a shady one other days» What is the best answer? | To stay at a comfortable temperature, warmer or cooler | Bats like to sunbathe for a tan | They follow the cat around the garden and sleep wherever it sleeps | Sunny boxes are always full of food
test: A man finds bats sleeping in a cold cellar in January and wakes them to look. Why is this harmful? | Waking uses up fat they need to survive the rest of winter | It makes them grow too fast | It stops them flying for ever | It gives them a nasty cold
exam: Identify the means by which the common and soprano pipistrelles were first distinguished as separate species. | By a difference in the pitch of their echolocation calls | By a difference in the colour of their wings | By a difference in the number of their digits | By a difference in the season of their flight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protection of roosting bats in Britain. | It is unlawful to disturb roosting bats or to inspect a bat box without a licence | It is lawful for any person to open a bat box at any time | Bats are protected solely during the winter months | The law protects bats only within national parks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the appropriate response upon finding a grounded bat in daylight? | The bat should not be handled with bare hands, and an adult should contact a bat helpline or veterinary surgeon | The bat should be lifted by hand and released from a height | The bat should be fed milk and kept as a pet | The bat should be left exposed to the sun until it recovers
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the loss of a single summer roost may harm a local bat population. | Females gather there to raise usually a single young per year, so losses are slowly replaced | Bats lay numerous eggs which require a specific roof | Bats are unable to fly without the shelter of a building | Roosts are the sole location in which bats are able to feed
